        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Hi @justinmk  ,\r\n\r\nI thoroughly looked at it again;\r\nI don't think it's possible without introducing the boolean.\r\n\r\nCurrently: starting treesitter disables syntax just once (immediately).\r\nHowever, it is possible for the user to re-enable the syntax later (even if treesitter is enabled)\r\n=> anything not highlighted by Treesitter can be highlighted by the syntax variable styling in this mode (I believe)\r\n\r\nIn this case we don't want to keep clearing the syntax variable every time the parser finishes, that would be bad.\r\n\r\ncurrent_syntax unfortunately provides no good indication of the state, the user can (re-)configure it however he likes, regardless of treesitter being done or not. I looked at some other alternatives, but in the end they added more complications then they solved (and I was worried it might break #36503 ).\r\n\r\nI gave the function/variable a clearer name\r\n& managed to slightly clean up the code (compared to master the diff is smaller and cleaner)\r\n\r\nShould still look through the CLI output and fix any failing ones..",
